    "case_summary": "A 75-year-old Hawaiian-Chinese female with a history of aortic stenosis, chronic renal insufficiency, and hypertension presented with pitting edema in both lower extremities. On March 21st, 2005, a cardiac catheterization revealed an ejection fraction of 15%, severe aortic stenosis, aortic regurgitation, and mitral regurgitation. During the procedure, venous and arterial sheaths were flushed with approximately 250 units of heparin each. Ten days later, she underwent elective aortic valve replacement and mitral valve repair surgery with intraaortic balloon pump (IABP) placement. During cardiopulmonary bypass, she received 32,000 units of heparin. Her preoperative platelet count of 108 × 10^9/L dropped to 25 × 10^9/L by the third postoperative day. She was given 12 units of random donor platelets and norepinephrine for blood pressure support. The decrease in platelets was linked to the IABP, which was subsequently removed. The patient experienced bleeding from the femoral insertion site and further hypotension, necessitating surgical repair of the femoral artery. During this surgery, she received 18 units of random donor platelets, 8 units of packed red blood cells, and 4 units of fresh frozen plasma. Her renal function worsened, requiring continuous venovenous hemodialysis (CVVHD). A heparin-flushed dialysis catheter was placed, and additional heparin was used in the CVVHD tubing circuit. Seven days postoperatively, the platelet count remained low (43 × 10^9/L) despite a total of 48 units of random donor platelets transfused. Differential diagnostic considerations included accelerated platelet removal due to CVVHD and sepsis-related disseminated intravascular coagulation. Two days later, cyanosis of the right hand was observed and attributed to a right radial arterial catheter, which was removed the next day without improvement. By then, severe ischemic changes were noted in all toes and fingers. Two days later, the platelet count reached its lowest point (8 × 10^9/L), leading to additional platelet transfusions.\n\nHeparin-platelet factor 4 enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) proved strongly positive.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
